-- Idyll's standard library, laid out as Meadow's is.
--
-- Every package has it, as `Std`, with no `[dependencies]` line, and none
-- of it is in scope until a `use` brings it: `use Std.Collections.List
-- (map, foldr)`, or `Std.Num.Nat.plus` written out in full. Its modules:
--
--   Function          id, const, flip, compose, ...
--   Bool              not, and, or, xor, implies
--   Ordering          Less, Equal, Greater, and comparing in turn
--   Cmp               comparing by a function passed along: minBy, maxBy,
--                     comparing, lexicographic, ...
--   Num.Int           abs, max, min, compare, pow, gcd, ...
--   Num.Nat           the natural numbers, which types compute with, and
--                     proofs about them
--   Num.Fin           Fin n, the naturals below n: safe indices
--   Eq                propositional equality: Refl, sym, trans, cong, subst
--   Void              the empty type, absurd, neg
--   Dec               decidable propositions: a proof, or a proof there is
--                     none
--   Maybe             what the prelude's Maybe needs
--   Result            and its Result
--   Either            one of two: Left or Right
--   Tuple             on tuples, (a, b): the prelude's Pair a b
--   Sigma             dependent pairs: a value, and something whose type
--                     depends on it
--   Collections.List  lists, and indexing them safely with a Fin
--   Collections.Vec   vectors, whose length is in their type
--   Collections.Array arrays, the rest of what the prelude declares; and
--                     indices whose type says they are in range
--   Collections.MArray
--                     arrays used linearly, so always changed in place
--   Collections.Vector
--                     Meadow's persistent RRB vector
--   Sort              a stable merge sort, of lists, arrays and vectors
--   String            building strings
--   Console           printing more than a line
--   Fs, Process       files, arguments and the environment, beyond the
--                     prelude's effects
--   Exn               exceptions, as an effect: throw, toResult, catch
--   State             a state, as an effect: get, put, and running with one
--   Yield             generators, as an effect: yield, and collecting a list
--
-- Linearity runs through all of it: a function takes what it uses once as
-- it is, what it may drop or only look at with the grade [0..1], and what it
-- uses many times with []. So `List.map` takes its function with [] (it is
-- called for each element) and its list as it is (each element is used
-- once), and `List.length` takes its list with [0..1], since it drops the
-- elements. Proofs take what only the types need with [0]. The collections
-- that share their parts, arrays and vectors, are taken with [], and so is
-- what they hand a function they are given.
